    "summary": "High‑voltage early‑'70 set with scorching Lovelight and tight Speedway jam",
    "blurb": "The night erupts from a sweet acoustic opening into a nonstop electric second set. Highlights include a blistering Turn On Your Lovelight, relentless New Speedway Boogie/Nobody’s Fault jam, and the first appearance of Attics Of My Life. Jerry’s guitar is fierce and Pigpen’s harmonica drives the momentum.",
    "review": "May 14, 1970 at Merramac Community College captured one of the Grateful Dead’s most electrified early‑'70 outings. The band launches with a gentle acoustic stretch – Don’t Ease Me In, Friend Of The Devil and Deep Elem Blues – before diving head‑first into a half‑hour of high‑octane rock. Jerry Garcia’s lead work is described by reviewers as “punching you in the guts,” his snarling Strat slicing through Pigpen’s wailing harmonica on Turn On Your Lovelight. The second set never lets up: a ferocious New Speedway Boogie segues into a sprawling Nobody’s Fault But Mine jam, then rockets through Saint Stephen, Not Fade Away and a raging Lovelight that even earned the nickname “Rock the Pig” in one tape.  Highlights also include the debut of Attics Of My Life, a solid Friend Of The Devil, and a soulful Candyman. While a few recordings note occasional hiss or drop‑outs, the consensus is that the performance itself is tight, energetic, and quintessentially early‑'70 Dead – a must‑listen for fans who love Jerry’s fierce guitar and the band’s relentless drive.",
